      <h2>How to Calculate a Percentage</h2>
      <p>
        There are three primary mathematical questions people ask when computing percentages:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>1. What is X% of Y?</strong> Formula: <code>(X ÷ 100) × Y</code>. Example: 20% of 500 = <code>(20 ÷ 100) × 500 = 100</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>2. X is what percent of Y?</strong> Formula: <code>(X ÷ Y) × 100</code>. Example: 50 out of 200 = <code>(50 ÷ 200) × 100 = 25%</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>3. Percentage Change (Increase/Decrease):</strong> Formula: <code>((New Value - Old Value) ÷ |Old Value|) × 100</code>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Marks Percentage Calculator & Grade Percentage Calculator</h2>
      <p>
        Our dedicated <strong>marks percentage calculator</strong> (also searched as <em>percentage calculator marks</em>, <em>mark percentage calculator</em>, or <em>percentage calculator of marks</em>) helps students, teachers, and parents calculate academic percentages and letter grades:
      </p>
      
      <div class="my-4 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-100/80 p-4 font-mono text-sm dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
        <div class="font-bold text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">Marks Percentage Formula:</div>
        <div class="mt-1">Percentage (%) = (Marks Obtained ÷ Total Maximum Marks) × 100</div>
      </div>

      <p>
        <strong>Example:</strong> If a student scores <strong>450 marks out of 500</strong>:
        <br />
        <code>(450 ÷ 500) × 100 = 90.00% (Grade A+)</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use the <strong>Multi-Subject Mode</strong> above to add individual subjects (Math, Science, English, etc.) dynamically to calculate your combined aggregate score and overall division.
      </p>

      <h2>CGPA to Percentage Calculator</h2>
      <p>
        Converting Cumulative Grade Point Average (CGPA) into an equivalent percentage is essential for college admissions, scholarship applications, and job eligibility.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Important:</strong> There is no single universal formula for all institutions worldwide. Different boards and universities use specific multipliers:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>CBSE Standard:</strong> <code>Percentage = CGPA × 9.5</code> (e.g., 8.2 CGPA = 77.90%).</li>
        <li><strong>10-Point Scale Standard:</strong> <code>Percentage = CGPA × 10.0</code> (e.g., 8.2 CGPA = 82.00%).</li>
        <li><strong>AICTE Formula:</strong> <code>Percentage = (CGPA - 0.75) × 10</code> (e.g., 8.2 CGPA = 74.50%).</li>
        <li><strong>Custom Multiplier:</strong> Allows you to enter your institution's specific multiplier.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Attendance Percentage Calculator & Target Tracker</h2>
      <p>
        Colleges and universities often enforce a mandatory minimum attendance criterion (typically <strong>75%</strong> or <strong>80%</strong>) to be eligible to sit for semester examinations.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our <strong>attendance percentage calculator</strong> not only computes your current attendance percentage (<code>Classes Attended ÷ Total Classes × 100</code>) but also determines the exact number of future consecutive classes you must attend to achieve your target threshold.
      </p>

      <h2>Hike Percentage Calculator</h2>
      <p>
        When evaluating job offers, appraisals, or annual salary revisions, the <strong>hike percentage calculator</strong> helps you determine the exact percentage increase:
      </p>
      <div class="my-4 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-100/80 p-4 font-mono text-sm dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
        <div class="font-bold text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">Salary Hike Formula:</div>
        <div class="mt-1">Hike (%) = ((New Salary - Current Salary) ÷ Current Salary) × 100</div>
      </div>
      <p>
        <strong>Example:</strong> If your current salary is <strong>$50,000</strong> and your new salary is <strong>$65,000</strong>:
        <br />
        <code>Hike = (($65,000 - $50,000) ÷ $50,000) × 100 = 30.00% hike ($15,000 annual / $1,250 monthly)</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>Increase Percentage Calculator vs Percentage Difference</h2>
      <p>
        It is vital to distinguish between <strong>Percentage Increase</strong>, <strong>Percentage Decrease</strong>, and <strong>Percentage Difference</strong>:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Percentage Increase:</strong> Measures growth from a specific original starting value to a higher new value: <code>((New - Old) ÷ Old) × 100</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Percentage Decrease:</strong> Measures reduction from an original baseline to a lower value: <code>((Old - New) ÷ Old) × 100</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Percentage Difference:</strong> Used when comparing two numbers where neither number is the "starting" baseline: <code>(|V1 - V2| ÷ ((V1 + V2) ÷ 2)) × 100</code>.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Body Fat Percentage Calculator</h2>
      <p>
        The <strong>body fat percentage calculator</strong> uses the official U.S. Navy circumference method (based on height, neck, waist, and hip measurements) to estimate body composition:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Essential Fat:</strong> 2–5% (Men) | 10–13% (Women)</li>
        <li><strong>Athletes:</strong> 6–13% (Men) | 14–20% (Women)</li>
        <li><strong>Fitness:</strong> 14–17% (Men) | 21–24% (Women)</li>
        <li><strong>Average:</strong> 18–24% (Men) | 25–31% (Women)</li>
        <li><strong>Obese:</strong> 25%+ (Men) | 32%+ (Women)</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Weight Loss Percentage Calculator</h2>
      <p>
        Tracking weight loss in percentages is far more meaningful than tracking raw pounds or kilograms alone, because a 10 kg loss represents 10% of a 100 kg person, but 14.3% of a 70 kg person.
        <br />
        <code>Weight Loss (%) = ((Starting Weight - Current Weight) ÷ Starting Weight) × 100</code>. If current weight is higher, our calculator automatically identifies and computes <strong>Weight Gain</strong>.
      </p>

      <h2>Win Percentage Calculator</h2>
      <p>
        Commonly used in sports leagues (NFL, NBA, Premier League, IPL) and gaming stats:
        <br />
        <code>Win Percentage (%) = ((Wins + 0.5 × Draws) ÷ Total Games Played) × 100</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>Average Percentage Calculator</h2>
      <p>
        When calculating the average of multiple percentages:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Standard Arithmetic Mean:</strong> Applicable when all sets have identical sample sizes or equal credits: <code>Sum of % ÷ Count</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Weighted Average:</strong> Essential when subjects or groups have different weightages or credits (e.g. 4-credit course vs 2-credit course): <code>Sum of (Value × Weight) ÷ Total Weights</code>.</li>
      </ul>

